# XBD 9.3 Basic Regular Expressions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# POSIX locale only. The bracket-expression rules (9.3.5) are shared with
# EREs (9.4.5), so tests 007 to 013 run with and without REG_EXTENDED and
# serve both sections. Not required, hence not tested: \? \+ \| (implementation
# defined), anchors inside a subexpression (optional), a lone [, adjacent
# duplication symbols, multi-character collating elements, ranges whose
# endpoints are equivalence classes.

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       Back-references \1 to \9 all work; the nth group is the one whose \(
           comes nth, nesting included: a pattern of nine groups followed by \9
           down to \1 matches abcdefghiihgfedcba with each group one character, and
           \(a\(b\(c\)\)\)\3\2\1 matches abccbcabc.
test       t/001-backref-digits.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       A back-reference followed by an asterisk or an interval expression is
           repeated like any single-item BRE: \(a\)\1* on aaaa takes all four, \(ab\)\1\{2\}
           matches ababab but not abab, and \(.\)\1\{2,\} on xaaaay takes the four
           a's but does not match xaay.
test       t/002-backref-dup.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       A subexpression repeated by an asterisk or interval takes no null
           iteration beyond its only match or what a minimum count needs: \(a*\)* on
           aab has group one at offsets 0 to 2, on b the empty string at 0, \(a*\)*c
           on aac group one 0 to 2, \(ab*\)* on abbab group one 3 to 5, and
           \(x*\)\{2\} matches x and the empty string.
test       t/003-star-null.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.7
class      required
since      1992
says       Precedence of a BRE: duplication binds to the item before it (ab\{2\} is a
           then two b's, a\.*b, a\**b, [ab]*c, a\(b\)*c), an escaped character
           and a bracket expression are single items (\[a] matches the three
           characters [a]), and anchoring has lower precedence than concatenation
           and duplication (^ab*, ab*$).
test       t/004-precedence.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.8
class      required
since      1992
says       A lone circumflex matches the empty string at offset 0 and a lone dollar
           sign the empty string at the end of the string, also for the empty
           string; the anchors also work around a subexpression (^\(a\), \(a\)$) and
           with repetitions (x*$ on axx is 1 to 3, ^x*).
test       t/005-anchors-lone.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       An interval expression accepts counts up to {RE_DUP_MAX} (capped at
           65536 in the test): a\{M\}, a\{0,M\} and a\{M,\} compile and match an
           anchored run of exactly M letters a and fail on one less or one more
           (where the form is exact). The limit is from <limits.h> or sysconf().
timeout    120
test       t/006-dup-max.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In a bracket expression (BRE and ERE) a closing bracket first in the list
           (after any circumflex) is a member, [a]b] is the list [a] followed by the
           characters b and ], [^^] excludes only the circumflex, a closing bracket
           written as the collating symbol [.].] does not end the list, and a
           left bracket not followed by a period, equals sign or colon is an ordinary
           member.
test       t/007-bracket-close.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.2
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale (BRE and ERE) ranges follow code order: a single-
           element range, several ranges in one list, a range after which a hyphen
           ends the list, collating symbols as one or both endpoints, a range from a
           space to a tilde and a range starting with a hyphen all match exactly the
           characters from start to end inclusive, and a non-matching list the rest.
test       t/008-ranges.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       [a--@] (a range from a to hyphen-minus is reversed) is either rejected
           by regcomp() or equivalent to a list holding only @; accepted, it matches
           @ and none of a, hyphen-minus, b, comma or a grave accent. Same in a BRE
           and an ERE.
test       t/009-reversed-hyphen-range.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       A non-matching list matches exactly the bytes 1 to 255 that the same list
           without the circumflex does not, in a BRE and an ERE, for lists built from
           plain characters, a range, a character class, a collating symbol, an
           equivalence class, a leading closing bracket, a leading or trailing
           hyphen, a trailing circumflex and a backslash.
test       t/010-complement.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale a collating symbol [.c.] written for an ordinary
           printable character (letters, digits and punctuation other than period,
           brackets, circumflex, hyphen and backslash) matches that character and no
           other byte, in a BRE and an ERE.
test       t/011-collating-symbols.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale an equivalence class [=c=] written for a printable
           character (letters, digits and punctuation other than equals sign,
           brackets, circumflex, hyphen and backslash) matches that character and no
           other byte, in a BRE and an ERE.
test       t/012-equivalence.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.013
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.2
class      required
since      1992
says       One list can hold ordinary characters, ranges, collating symbols,
           equivalence classes and character classes together, with a closing
           bracket first and a hyphen last, and means the union of them (BRE and ERE),
           its non-matching form the complement.
test       t/013-mixed-list.c

assertion  XBD.9.3-bre.014
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       Enclosing a BRE in \( and \), once or nested up to twenty deep, does not
           change what it matches: the offsets of the whole match are the same as
           without the groups for ab*, [ab]*c, a.c, a*, x\.y and \(a\)* on a set of
           strings, and re_nsub counts the groups.
test       t/014-group-transparent.c
